# The family's MSBuild switches. Copy this file to the root of a repository that takes the build
# kit, beside Directory.Build.props. MSBuild finds it by walking up from the directory of the
# project being built, whatever directory the build starts from, and reads it before the command
# line, so a switch given on the command line wins over anything here.

# Node reuse cannot be set from a props file: MSBuild decides it before any project is evaluated,
# which is why this switch lives in a response file and not beside the kit's other defaults.

# End the worker nodes with the build rather than keeping them alive for the next one. Node reuse
# is on unless a build turns it off, and the .NET CLI does not turn it off. A worker node is
# launched with CREATE_NO_WINDOW, which allocates a console all the same, so every node also holds
# a console host. Left to reuse them, a solution build leaves one worker node and one console host
# per node behind for fifteen minutes — fifteen of each and about 2 GB on a sixteen-core machine —
# and concurrent builds stack them. Ending them with the build costs no measurable time: a clean
# solution build takes the same either way.
-nodeReuse:false

# A worker cap does not belong here: the .NET CLI passes its own -maxcpucount on the command line,
# which beats an -m written here, so a cap set here does nothing. The override is switch by switch
# and not file-wide — the CLI passes no node-reuse switch, so the switch above decides.

# Whether this file works is read off what is still running once a build has exited, never off a
# worker count taken while it runs: node reuse changes when the workers end, not how many there are.
